1️⃣ TCP vs UDP (with DNS reasoning)
TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)

Reliable, ordered, connection-oriented

3-way handshake (SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK)

Guarantees delivery

Retransmits lost packets

Slower but safe

Used when correctness matters

HTTP/HTTPS

Email

File transfers

UDP (User Datagram Protocol)

Fast, connectionless, no guarantee

No handshake

No retransmission

Packets may arrive out of order or be lost

Very low latency

Used when speed matters

DNS

Video streaming

Online gaming

VoIP

🔥 Why DNS uses UDP?

DNS queries are small

Speed matters more than reliability

If packet is lost → client retries

UDP avoids handshake overhead

👉 But: DNS switches to TCP when:

Response > 512 bytes

Zone transfers

DNSSEC

💡 Interview line:

“DNS primarily uses UDP for speed, but falls back to TCP when reliability or larger payloads are required.”

2️⃣ DNS Resolution (Step-by-Step)

When you type www.google.com:

Browser cache
→ Already resolved? Done.

OS cache

Router / ISP cache

Recursive Resolver (ISP / public like 8.8.8.8)
If not cached:

Root DNS Server
→ “I don’t know google.com, but ask .com servers”

TLD Server (.com)
→ “Ask Google’s authoritative server”

Authoritative DNS Server
→ Returns IP (e.g., 142.250.x.x)

Resolver caches it and returns IP to browser

💡 Key terms to drop:

Recursive resolver

Root / TLD / Authoritative

TTL (Time To Live)

